<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jadwiga of Poland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1374–<a href="/years/1399">1399</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Queen Jadwiga · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Capetian House of Anjou</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jadwiga was crowned ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the late fourteenth century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a> of 1385 she married the Lithuanian grand duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, who accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and became King Władysław II Jagiełło. Their marriage began the dynastic union of Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland.jpg" alt="Jadwiga of Poland" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jadwiga_Andegawe%C5%84ska_seal_1386.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jadwiga was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, the youngest of three daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> and Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, probably between 3 October 1373 and 18 February 1374. Both her grandmothers were Polish princesses, which linked her to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>. Louis had no sons, and in 1374 the Polish nobles accepted that one of his daughters would succeed him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after he confirmed their liberties in the Privilege of Koszyce. She was symbolically married to William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1378. When Louis died in 1382 her sister Mary was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, while the Polish nobles resolved to obey only a daughter of Louis who would settle in Poland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Jadwiga was crowned king, not queen, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 16 October 1384 by Archbishop Bodzanta. Scholars suggest the title marked her as a sovereign rather than a consort. The Polish lords refused to accept the fourteen-year-old William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, thinking he could not guard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> against its neighbours, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a>. The lords of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> proposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>, the pagan grand duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, whose envoys asked for her hand in January 1385. In August 1385 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a>, promising to convert with his kinsmen and subjects and to pay 200,000 florins to William. Polish legend says she agreed only after long prayer before a crucifix in Wawel Cathedral.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> was baptised Władysław in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in February 1386, and Jadwiga declared her union with William void. He was crowned as her co-ruler. Her real political power was limited, but after her mother was murdered in 1387 she went into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and persuaded most of its people to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>. She mediated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. In 1397 she issued a charter for a college for Lithuanian students in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jadwiga became pregnant in September 1398 and gave birth to a daughter at Wawel Castle on 22 June 1399. She died on 17 July 1399 of postpartum complications, and her daughter predeceased her. As the last hereditary monarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, her death made the throne elective. Six days after her funeral Władysław left for Ruthenia, and the lords sent envoys to renew their oaths to him; Jadwiga founded hospitals, schools and churches and encouraged hymns in Polish. The historians Oscar Halecki and S. Harrison Thomson rank her among the great rulers of Poland. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> canonised her in 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jadwiga of Poland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The union’s roots lay in the Union of Krewo of 1385, under which the Lithuanian Grand Duke Jogaila agreed to accept Latin Christianity and marry <b>Queen Jadwiga</b> of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Louis of Hungary, his daughter <b>Jadwiga</b> married Jogaila of Lithuania under the Union of Krewo of 1385, beginning the Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569).</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland (1025-1385)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1385 the Union of Krewo arranged the marriage of <b>Queen Jadwiga</b> to Jogaila, grand duke of Lithuania, who was baptised and crowned in 1386 as Wladyslaw II Jagiello.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.47,19.05&amp;year=1390" aria-label="Buda on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jadwiga of Poland"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4371 6011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4371 6011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.47,19.05&amp;year=1390">What was here in 1390? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
